# 8. Per-Slice Implementation Workflow

The factory uses a two-level gating loop (see `<factory-path>/docs/adr/0008-per-slice-and-per-phase-gating.md`). The shared task tracker is `TASKS.md`; the human review queue is `ESCALATIONS.md`.

## Your role in the loop

You are the **per-slice implementer**. You build one slice at a time, mark it `awaiting-review`, and stop. You do not start the next slice until Codex has approved the current one.

When you pick up a slice from `TASKS.md`:

1. Read the slice's acceptance criteria from `ARCHITECTURE.md` (Work Breakdown) and `CURSOR_HANDOFF.md`.
2. Read every `docs/adr/*.md` in the project for constraints that affect this slice.
3. Mark the slice `in-progress` in `TASKS.md` and set Owner to cursor.
4. Implement the slice end-to-end per the Cursor handoff: UI, API, integration, tests, docs as scoped.
5. Run local tests and confirm they pass.
6. Commit and open a PR (or stage the change per `<factory-path>/standards/git-workflow.md`).
7. Mark the slice `awaiting-review` in `TASKS.md`. **Stop.** Do not start the next slice.

## When Codex files sub-tasks

If the slice comes back with sub-tasks (status `in-progress`, iteration counter incremented):

1. Read every sub-task in `TASKS.md` for that slice.
2. Fix each sub-task with focused changes. Do not refactor unrelated code.
3. Re-run tests, commit, mark the slice `awaiting-review` again.
4. Increment the iteration counter; do not push past the cap.

## Budget caps

| Cap | Default |
|---|---|
| Per-task iterations | 3 |
| Per-session token cap | 100,000 |
| Per-session wall time | 30 minutes |

Read the project's `TASKS.md` header for any overrides.

## When you hit a cap

Stop. Do not keep trying. Append an entry to `ESCALATIONS.md` with:

- Slice identifier
- Reason: `iteration-cap-hit`, `external-dependency`, `secret-needed`, or `other`
- What was tried (each iteration summarized)
- Recommended action for the product owner

Mark the affected slice as `human-needed` in `TASKS.md`. Do not start any other slice that depends on this one.

## When you need a secret or external resource

Do not invent placeholder values that look real. Append an `ESCALATIONS.md` entry with reason `secret-needed` or `external-dependency`, mark the slice `blocked` in `TASKS.md`, and stop. The product owner provides the resource or escalates to the vendor.

## When you spot an architecture problem

Do not silently change the design. Follow the escalation protocol in `<factory-path>/CLAUDE.md` Section 12:

1. Stop work on the affected slice.
2. Mark it `blocked` in `TASKS.md` with a note pointing at the architecture concern.
3. Surface the concern to the architect (Claude) — in Stage 1, route it through the product owner; in Stage 2, the orchestrator routes it.
4. Wait for Claude to amend the architecture (new ADR) or push back with reasoning before resuming.
